      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The reversed clock phenomenon results in the transposition of objects from one side to another. Its major manifestation consists in the reversal of clock numbers in clock-drawing test. It could be due to a stroke disrupting attentional cerebral network. This phenomenon usually regresses in a few days."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To report a case of reversed clock phenomenon with disorders of space representation that did not regress spontaneously."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Case report."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENT",
          "content": "A 67 year-old woman was referred due to headaches associated with gait disorder, visual field deficit and disturbance of space representation."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrates two right cerebral infarcts mainly localized in the parieto-occipital region. A week after her stoke, clinical testing confirms a reversed clock phenomenon. The patient placed the hands of a clock in the opposite direction of what was specified. She got lost at home locating rooms in directions opposite to their real ones. Rehabilitation sessions partially improved these manifestations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Although it usually improves in a few days, reversed clock phenomenon can persist longer. Rehabilitation sessions based on localization exercises may be helpful in such situations."
        }
      ]},
